WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.322
This practice asks you to keep every employee row while adding department and company averages.

00:00:06.322 --> 00:00:10.517
That wording tells us not to collapse the result with GROUP BY.

00:00:10.517 --> 00:00:16.817
Instead, use window functions. One average should use a department partition.

00:00:16.817 --> 00:00:20.202
The other should use the full set of remaining rows.

00:00:20.202 --> 00:00:26.612
Run the starter example after completing it and inspect several employees from the same department.

00:00:26.612 --> 00:00:32.307
Their department average should match, while their individual salary values remain different.

00:00:32.307 --> 00:00:36.112
Then compare that with the company-wide average.

00:00:36.112 --> 00:00:40.637
This is a useful test for deciding between GROUP BY and windows:

00:00:40.637 --> 00:00:47.477
if the original detail rows must remain visible, a window function is often the better analytical tool.

00:00:47.477 --> 00:00:51.677
For this activity, make a prediction before you run anything.

00:00:51.677 --> 00:00:56.737
State what one output row should represent, which records you expect to remain,

00:00:56.737 --> 00:01:00.297
and what edge case is most likely to expose a mistake.

00:01:00.297 --> 00:01:04.437
Then run the SQL and compare the result with that prediction.

00:01:04.437 --> 00:01:11.497
If it does not match, debug the logic in small pieces instead of changing several clauses at once.

00:01:11.497 --> 00:01:15.667
This is the same habit you would use in a real analytics project:

00:01:15.667 --> 00:01:20.376
validate the meaning of the result, not only whether the statement executes.
